WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.460
38 Dutch large coppers released at WoodWalton Fenn in 1927 required annual captive releases for over 60 years. This is unintended consequences, episode 147. In the summer of 1928, more than a thousand adult large coppers flew across the sedge and pools of WoodWalton Fenn. That flight looked like a restoration. The year before, Captain E.B. Purefoy had brought Dutch butterflies to a small national trust reserve and planted their food plant,

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:35.740
great water dock in advance. The insects answered. Eggs and larvae appeared on the dock leaves

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:41.140
that same season. Everyone involved took the count as confirmation that a lost butterfly could

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:46.860
be returned to a fragment of English fen. The colony did not hold on its own. Wardens kept

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:51.880
breeding fresh adults and releasing them every season, while the farmland around the reserve kept

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:58.140
draining the fen dryer. What began as a careful act of repair became a population that existed only

00:00:58.140 --> 00:00:59.980
because people kept putting it back.

00:01:00.828 --> 00:01:06.908
The English race of the large copper, lysena, despair, despair, had disappeared by 1851.

00:01:07.608 --> 00:01:10.248
The cause was not a mystery to the people who came after.

00:01:10.868 --> 00:01:15.088
Centuries of fend drainage for agriculture had taken the wetlands the butterfly needed.

00:01:15.648 --> 00:01:19.928
By the early 20th century, only fragments of wetland remained in eastern England.

00:01:20.488 --> 00:01:21.848
The original race was gone.

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:24.788
The landscape that had held it was mostly fields.

00:01:24.788 --> 00:01:27.728
Purfoy was an experienced lepidopterist.

00:01:28.028 --> 00:01:32.248
He looked at those fragments and saw a second chance in a newly-discovered Dutch race,

00:01:32.768 --> 00:01:34.208
Lysena Despair Batavis.

00:01:34.828 --> 00:01:37.328
The Dutch insect was not the lost English one.

00:01:37.788 --> 00:01:42.308
It was close enough in his judgment to stand in for a butterfly England no longer had.

00:01:42.868 --> 00:01:47.388
Wood-Walton Fenn, in Huntingdonshire, still held pockets of suitable vegetation.

00:01:48.068 --> 00:01:51.188
It was small, it was also protected, which mattered.

00:01:51.188 --> 00:01:55.388
A national trust boundary meant the ground inside it would not be plowed.

00:01:55.868 --> 00:02:00.108
Purfoy and the reserve managers believed that preparing the site with the larval food

00:02:00.108 --> 00:02:04.428
plant would let a self-sustaining colony establish itself inside that boundary.

00:02:04.988 --> 00:02:07.408
The reasoning followed the best available knowledge.

00:02:07.928 --> 00:02:10.888
The butterflies extinction had been caused by habitat loss.

00:02:11.528 --> 00:02:15.308
Targeted habitat restoration, plus fresh stock, should reverse it.

00:02:15.748 --> 00:02:17.348
That logic is worth sitting with.

00:02:17.348 --> 00:02:20.028
If the plant is missing, put the plant back.

00:02:20.568 --> 00:02:23.088
If the insect is missing, bring the insect back.

00:02:23.588 --> 00:02:26.448
If the ground is protected, the work has a place to stand.

00:02:26.968 --> 00:02:31.568
The drainage that removed the original English population had begun in the 17th century.

00:02:32.288 --> 00:02:33.748
Large, straight channels were cut.

00:02:34.368 --> 00:02:35.928
Wind-powered pumps were installed.

00:02:36.488 --> 00:02:41.228
By the 19th century, steam engines accelerated the lowering of water levels across hundreds

00:02:41.228 --> 00:02:42.088
of square miles.

00:02:42.088 --> 00:02:47.748
peat soils became arable fields. Purefoy was therefore working with a landscape whose

00:02:47.748 --> 00:02:52.668
hydrology had already been altered for more than 200 years. He could see that the remaining

00:02:52.668 --> 00:02:57.548
fragments were small. He also knew that the Dutch race had evolved in similar, if not

00:02:57.548 --> 00:03:02.708
identical, wetland conditions. The decision to proceed rested on an assumption that still

00:03:02.708 --> 00:03:07.648
sounds reasonable. A carefully chosen reserve could be made sufficient if the missing plant

00:03:07.648 --> 00:03:13.688
was restored. The reserve was the unit he could act on. The water under it was not a separate problem

00:03:13.688 --> 00:03:19.657
in the plan. It was treated as part of the ground the boundary already held. In 1927,

00:03:19.917 --> 00:03:26.017
Purfoy released 25 males and 13 females of the Dutch race at Woodwalt and Fen. The reserve had

00:03:26.017 --> 00:03:31.897
already been planted with great water dock. The planting set out hundreds of young Rumex hydro-lapathom

00:03:31.897 --> 00:03:37.357
along the wetter margins of the reserve's internal waterways. Purfoy chose locations where the

00:03:37.357 --> 00:03:39.597
Pete remained saturated for most of the year.

00:03:40.097 --> 00:03:43.297
Those spots matched the conditions he had observed in Dutch colonies.

00:03:43.837 --> 00:03:47.337
The following year, more than 1,000 adults were counted on the wing.

00:03:48.097 --> 00:03:51.937
Learn butterflies records that the early numbers encouraged everyone involved.

00:03:52.397 --> 00:03:55.817
The count represented roughly 27 times the number of founders.

00:03:56.457 --> 00:04:01.117
An apparent multiplication of that size suggested that the restored food plant and the protected

00:04:01.117 --> 00:04:03.417
status of the Fenn were together sufficient.

00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:07.537
Warden's recorded eggs and larvae on the dock leaves that season.

00:04:07.957 --> 00:04:11.677
The sense on the evidence in front of them was that the cycle had closed.

00:04:12.257 --> 00:04:14.557
Reserve staff continued modest management.

00:04:15.117 --> 00:04:19.057
They kept the food plant abundant inside the fence existing ditches and pools.

00:04:19.737 --> 00:04:24.397
No one at the time expected that the colony would require repeated human interventions simply

00:04:24.397 --> 00:04:24.937
to persist.

00:04:25.537 --> 00:04:27.297
The encouragement was not vanity.

00:04:27.797 --> 00:04:32.357
A small release had produced a large flight, and the life stages were visible on the plant

00:04:32.357 --> 00:04:33.697
that had been put there for them.

00:04:34.377 --> 00:04:38.517
A lepidopterist and a set of wardens could look at that summer and believe the hard part

00:04:38.517 --> 00:04:38.997
was done.

00:04:40.072 --> 00:04:44.472
The land beyond Wood Walton Fenn's boundaries had been progressively drained for arable

00:04:44.472 --> 00:04:46.352
farming since the 17th century.

00:04:46.952 --> 00:04:50.412
Each new pump and each new ditch lowered the regional water table.

00:04:51.032 --> 00:04:55.372
Moisture was pulled out of the reserve through the peat, whether or not anyone inside the

00:04:55.372 --> 00:04:56.312
fence intended it.

00:04:56.812 --> 00:04:59.572
Great water dock needs consistently wet conditions.

00:04:59.572 --> 00:05:04.232
As the table fell, the plant became harder to maintain in sufficient density.

00:05:04.992 --> 00:05:10.712
Duffy's studies and the records kept at the reserve show that natural recruitment soon fell below replacement levels.

00:05:11.292 --> 00:05:16.832
The butterflies were still there. The next generation on its own was not replacing the one before it.

00:05:17.452 --> 00:05:22.652
Wardens responded by rearing butterflies in a greenhouse and releasing batches of adults each summer.

00:05:22.652 --> 00:05:28.412
The practice continued for decades, larvae were also caged on selected plants to protect

00:05:28.412 --> 00:05:30.092
them from flooding or from drought.

00:05:30.532 --> 00:05:33.192
These measures kept a visible population alive.

00:05:33.752 --> 00:05:36.832
The colony never became independent of the annual supplements.

00:05:37.312 --> 00:05:41.692
By the late 1960s, a major flood further damaged the food plant stands.

00:05:42.152 --> 00:05:46.212
That damage prompted another round of captive rearing after 1969.

00:05:46.912 --> 00:05:49.952
The surrounding agricultural drainage continued unchanged.

00:05:49.952 --> 00:05:53.092
The hydrological deficit grew rather than shrank.

00:05:53.692 --> 00:05:56.052
The second order effect was quiet and cumulative.

00:05:56.772 --> 00:06:00.212
Each year of a lowered water table reduced the area of suitable dock.

00:06:00.832 --> 00:06:03.292
The third order effect followed from that shrinkage.

00:06:03.632 --> 00:06:08.392
A smaller stand of dock increased the proportion of the population that had to be artificially

00:06:08.392 --> 00:06:08.892
supported.

00:06:09.312 --> 00:06:14.112
Without that support, the numbers would have collapsed far earlier than the early 1990s,

00:06:14.332 --> 00:06:16.372
when the last supplemented adults were seen.

00:06:16.372 --> 00:06:22.652
the peat itself compounded the difficulty. Once drained from the outside, the organic soil began to

00:06:22.652 --> 00:06:28.392
oxidize and subside. Substitents lowered the surface of the reserve relative to the surrounding fields.

00:06:28.892 --> 00:06:34.392
Water became even harder to hold at the level the dock required. Every additional pump installed on

00:06:34.392 --> 00:06:40.212
neighboring farms increased the gradient. Water was drawn through the peat more rapidly. The warden's

00:06:40.212 --> 00:06:45.212
greenhouse rearing addressed the shortage of adult butterflies. The underlying shortage of suitable

00:06:45.212 --> 00:06:51.392
larval habitat continued to widen. Caging larvae protected individuals from immediate weather extremes.

00:06:52.132 --> 00:06:56.872
Caging could not expand the total area of dock that stayed wet long enough for the caterpillars

00:06:56.872 --> 00:07:01.592
to complete their development. The arithmetic of the intervention shifted year by year.

00:07:02.132 --> 00:07:06.152
More adults had to be reared to compensate for fewer naturally occurring plants.

00:07:06.832 --> 00:07:11.072
Picture the work from the warden's side of the ditch, a greenhouse, a batch of adults,

00:07:11.072 --> 00:07:14.572
a cage over a plant that might otherwise be drowned or dried.

00:07:15.172 --> 00:07:19.932
Each of those acts kept something alive that the fenn as it then stood could not keep

00:07:19.932 --> 00:07:20.672
alive alone.

00:07:21.372 --> 00:07:24.472
None of those acts raised the water table on the farm's next door.

00:07:25.132 --> 00:07:29.632
The pumps kept running, the peat kept losing height, the wet margin kept narrowing.

00:07:30.192 --> 00:07:34.552
A visitor in a good summer could still see large coppers on the wing and conclude that

00:07:34.552 --> 00:07:36.652
the 1927 release had worked.

00:07:37.252 --> 00:07:39.452
The books in the greenhouse told a different story.

00:07:39.452 --> 00:07:44.772
The flight was being restocked, that gap between what the eye saw and what the rearing records

00:07:44.772 --> 00:07:47.952
required is the heart of the unintended consequence.

00:07:48.512 --> 00:07:50.772
Protection of the fragment produced a population.

00:07:51.372 --> 00:07:54.072
It did not produce a population that could replace itself.

00:07:54.772 --> 00:07:56.372
The good intention had been precise.

00:07:57.092 --> 00:08:01.912
Restore the plant, release the insect, stay inside a reserve that would not be farmed.

00:08:02.272 --> 00:08:06.772
The water that the plant required was still being removed by a drainage regime older than

00:08:06.772 --> 00:08:08.752
the reserve and larger than its boundary.

00:08:09.795 --> 00:08:15.835
After the final sightings in 1994 and 1995, the formal reintroduction program at Wood

00:08:15.835 --> 00:08:16.935
Walton Fenn ended.

00:08:17.515 --> 00:08:19.755
The site remained a national nature reserve.

00:08:20.375 --> 00:08:25.255
Managers accepted that a viable large copper population could not be sustained without restoring

00:08:25.255 --> 00:08:26.915
the wider Fenn hydrology.

00:08:27.395 --> 00:08:30.075
That acceptance is a change in the unit of the problem.

00:08:30.695 --> 00:08:33.555
The reserve was no longer treated as a sufficient container.

00:08:33.555 --> 00:08:36.515
The water regime around it had to move with it.

00:08:36.955 --> 00:08:42.495
The Great Fen project, now underway, aims to re-wet large areas of former farmland between

00:08:42.495 --> 00:08:48.215
Wood Walton and Home Fen. The tools are clay buns and controlled water levels, meant

00:08:48.215 --> 00:08:52.955
to recreate the conditions the butterfly once required. No new releases have occurred

00:08:52.955 --> 00:08:58.615
while the landscape scale work continues. The original 1927 effort therefore stands as

00:08:58.615 --> 00:09:03.275
a 60-year demonstration that protecting a single fragment is not enough when the surrounding

00:09:03.275 --> 00:09:08.895
drainage regime remains unaltered. The project's current phase involves purchasing arable land

00:09:08.895 --> 00:09:14.055
and installing impermeable barriers so that winter rainfall can be retained rather than pumped

00:09:14.055 --> 00:09:19.415
away. Those measures address the same water table gradient that had steadily reduced the area

00:09:19.415 --> 00:09:25.095
of great water dock inside the original reserve. Until the restored blocks reach a sufficient size

00:09:25.095 --> 00:09:30.195
and wetness, any reintroduced butterflies would face the same constraints that forced the earlier

00:09:30.195 --> 00:09:32.515
program into perpetual supplementation.

00:09:33.175 --> 00:09:35.575
The irony is not that the first plan was careless.

00:09:36.135 --> 00:09:38.435
The irony is that the rescue became the evidence.

00:09:39.075 --> 00:09:43.615
Sixty years of greenhouse rearing showed, more clearly than a failed first summer would have,

00:09:44.135 --> 00:09:45.915
that the boundary was the wrong edge.

00:09:46.175 --> 00:09:50.475
A collapse in 1929 might have been read as a bad stock or a bad season.

00:09:51.035 --> 00:09:55.875
A colony that could be seen year after year only because it was put back, pointed at

00:09:55.875 --> 00:09:57.315
the water leaving under the fence.

00:09:58.333 --> 00:10:03.693
Purfoy in the Wood Walton Wardens treated the reserve boundary as the decisive limit of their intervention.

00:10:04.273 --> 00:10:08.993
The result was a population that existed only inside annual budgets for captive rearing.

00:10:09.513 --> 00:10:13.113
The principle that follows is narrower than a slogan about thinking bigger.

00:10:13.733 --> 00:10:19.293
Any plan that sets a fixed geographical perimeter must also secure the water regime across that perimeter,

00:10:19.653 --> 00:10:21.633
or the food plant itself will disappear.

00:10:21.633 --> 00:10:27.053
year. What was measured, season after season, was adults on the wing and larvae on selected

00:10:27.053 --> 00:10:32.093
plants. What was not secured was the wetness those plants needed once the regional table

00:10:32.093 --> 00:10:37.913
fell and the peat subsided. Reward the visible insect and you will get a greenhouse. Secure

00:10:37.913 --> 00:10:42.253
the gradient that feeds the plant and the insect has a chance to stop being a budget line.

00:10:42.853 --> 00:10:47.373
When the next reintroduction is considered, the test will be whether the great fence restored

00:10:47.373 --> 00:10:51.313
water table can support great water dock without constant human rescue.

00:10:51.953 --> 00:10:56.513
The same question faces every recovery project that begins inside a remnant habitat

00:10:56.513 --> 00:10:58.573
ringed by intensified land use.

00:10:59.253 --> 00:11:03.373
Environmental intelligence has been tracking how restoration targets get drawn against

00:11:03.373 --> 00:11:08.573
working farmland, and the Wood Walton record is a long case of what those lines leave out.

00:11:09.133 --> 00:11:13.933
If you are watching a recovery that starts inside a reserve while pumps, canals, or ground

00:11:13.933 --> 00:11:16.393
water takes keep running on the land around it.

00:11:16.393 --> 00:11:18.813
What would you watch for in the second decade?

00:11:19.333 --> 00:11:20.393
Not the second summer.

00:11:20.973 --> 00:11:25.793
A cell for placing stand of the food plant or a population that only appears when someone

00:11:25.793 --> 00:11:27.113
opens the greenhouse door.

00:11:27.693 --> 00:11:28.813
We'll leave it there for today.

